WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:08.088
Everyone says you have to get ready
to retire financially.

00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:10.465
And of course you do.

00:00:10.507 --> 00:00:11.883
But what they don't tell you

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:15.804
is that you also have to get
ready psychologically.

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:17.973
Who knew?

00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:21.726
But it's important
for a couple of reasons.

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:27.524
First, 10,000 North Americans
will retire today

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:31.486
and every day for the next 10 to 15 years.

00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:35.365
This is a retirement tsunami.

00:00:35.407 --> 00:00:38.702
And when these folks
come crashing onto the beach,

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:42.789
a lot of them are going to feel
like fish out of water,

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:45.709
without a clue as to what to expect.

00:00:47.002 --> 00:00:50.964
Secondly, it's important
because there is a very good chance

00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:56.928
that you will live one third
of your life in retirement.

00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:00.348
So it's important that you have a heads up

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.894
to the fact that there will be
significant psychological changes

00:01:04.936 --> 00:01:07.147
and challenges that come with it.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.320
I belong to a walking group
that meets early three mornings a week.

00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:19.242
Our primary goal is to put 10,000
steps on our Fitbits,

00:01:19.284 --> 00:01:24.372
and then we go for coffee
and cinnamon buns more importantly.

00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:29.961
(Laughter and applause)

00:01:30.378 --> 00:01:32.922
So we've gotten the habit of, as we walk,

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:36.468
we've gotten the habit of choosing
a topic for discussion.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:39.179
And one day the topic was,

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.516
how do you squeeze all the juice
out of retirement?

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:47.062
How's that for seven o'clock
in the morning?

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:48.063
(Laughter)

00:01:48.146 --> 00:01:52.317
So we walk and we talk, and the next day
we go on to the next topic.

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:54.319
But the question stayed with me

00:01:54.361 --> 00:01:58.490
because I was really having
some challenges with retirement.

00:01:58.948 --> 00:02:00.450
I was busy enough,

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.869
but I really didn't feel
that I was doing very much

00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:06.081
that was significant or important.

00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:08.166
I was really struggling.

00:02:08.625 --> 00:02:11.544
I thought I had a pretty good idea
of what success looked like

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.463
in a working career,

00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:17.467
but when it came to retirement,
it was fuzzier for me.

00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:20.553
So I decided to dig deeper.

00:02:20.595 --> 00:02:24.724
And what I discovered was that much
of the material on retirement

00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:29.104
focuses on the financial
and/or the estate side of things.

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:31.481
And of course, they're both important,

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:33.900
but just not what I was looking for.

00:02:34.442 --> 00:02:38.488
So I interviewed dozens
and dozens of retirees

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.199
and I asked them the question:

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:44.911
how do you squeeze all the juice
out of retirement?

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:50.709
What I discovered was
that there is a framework

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.919
that can help make sense of it all.

00:02:53.294 --> 00:02:56.172
And that's what I want
to share with you today.

00:02:56.965 --> 00:03:00.260
You see, there are four distinct phases

00:03:00.301 --> 00:03:04.514
that most of us move through
in retirement.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:09.310
And as you'll see,
it's not always a smooth ride.

00:03:10.353 --> 00:03:11.813
In the next few minutes,

00:03:11.813 --> 00:03:16.317
you’ll recognize which phase
you’re in, if you’re retired.

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:17.527
And if you’re not,

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:21.239
you'll have a better idea
of what to expect when that time comes.

00:03:22.073 --> 00:03:26.786
And best of all, you'll know
that there is a phase four.

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:32.041
The most gratifying, satisfying
of the four phases,

00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:36.629
and that's where you can squeeze
all the juice out of retirement.

00:03:38.339 --> 00:03:43.136
Phase one is the vacation phase,
and that's just what it's like.

00:03:43.887 --> 00:03:48.266
You wake up when you want,
you do what you want all day.

00:03:48.308 --> 00:03:52.604
And the best part is
that there is no set routine.

00:03:53.354 --> 00:03:59.527
For most people, phase one represents
their view of an ideal retirement.

00:04:00.028 --> 00:04:04.783
Relaxing, fun in the sun, freedom, baby.

00:04:04.783 --> 00:04:05.950
(Laughter)

00:04:06.451 --> 00:04:10.497
And for most folks, phase one lasts
for about a year or so.

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:15.126
And then strangely,
it begins to lose its luster.

00:04:15.794 --> 00:04:18.213
We begin to feel a bit bored.

00:04:18.797 --> 00:04:21.466
We actually miss our routine.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.427
Something in us seems to need one.

00:04:25.053 --> 00:04:27.597
And we ask ourselves,

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:30.558
"Is that all there is to retirement?"

00:04:31.851 --> 00:04:35.688
Now when these thoughts and feelings
start to bubble up,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.733
you have already moved into phase two.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:47.367
Phase two is when we feel
loss and we feel lost.

00:04:48.952 --> 00:04:52.956
Phase two is when we lose the big five,

00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:57.544
significant losses
all associated with retirement.

00:04:58.294 --> 00:05:00.296
We lose that routine.

00:05:00.338 --> 00:05:02.298
We lose a sense of identity.

00:05:02.799 --> 00:05:06.803
We lose many of the relationships
that we had established at work.

00:05:07.262 --> 00:05:09.347
We lose a sense of purpose,

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:12.600
and for some people,
there's a loss of power.

00:05:13.476 --> 00:05:16.354
Now we don't see these things coming.

00:05:16.396 --> 00:05:20.441
We didn’t see these losses coming,
and because they happen all at once,

00:05:20.483 --> 00:05:23.236
it’s like, poof, gone.

00:05:23.778 --> 00:05:25.113
It's traumatic.

00:05:27.031 --> 00:05:32.704
Phase two is also when we come
face-to-face with the three Ds:

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.456
divorce,

00:05:35.498 --> 00:05:36.833
depression

00:05:36.875 --> 00:05:40.628
and decline, both physical and mental.

00:05:41.588 --> 00:05:42.881
The result of all of this

00:05:42.922 --> 00:05:46.134
is that we can feel
like we've been hit by a bus.

00:05:47.552 --> 00:05:53.099
You see, before we can appreciate
and enjoy some of the positive aspects

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:57.353
associated with phase three and four,

00:05:57.395 --> 00:06:01.983
you are going to, in phase two, feel fear,

00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:05.528
anxiety and quite even depression.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.448
That's just the way it is.

00:06:08.448 --> 00:06:12.035
So buckle up, get ready.

00:06:13.536 --> 00:06:16.956
Fortunately, at some point,
most of us say to ourselves,

00:06:16.998 --> 00:06:19.125
"Hey, I can't go on like this.

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:22.420
I don't want to spend the rest of my life,

00:06:22.462 --> 00:06:26.299
perhaps 30 years, feeling like this."

00:06:27.175 --> 00:06:31.804
And when we do, we've turned
a corner to phase three.

00:06:33.598 --> 00:06:36.935
Phase three is a time of trial and error.

00:06:37.477 --> 00:06:40.355
In phase three, we ask ourselves,

00:06:40.396 --> 00:06:44.025
“How can I make my life meaningful again?

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.278
How can I contribute?”

00:06:47.904 --> 00:06:54.243
The answer often is to do things
that you love to do and do really well.

00:06:55.203 --> 00:07:01.000
But phase three can also deliver
some disappointment and failure.

00:07:02.043 --> 00:07:04.545
For example,

00:07:04.587 --> 00:07:08.466
I spent a couple of years
serving on a condo board

00:07:08.508 --> 00:07:11.260
until I finally got tired
of being yelled at.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.804
(Laughter)

00:07:14.514 --> 00:07:19.894
You see, one year the board decided
that we were going to plant daffodils

00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:22.438
rather than the traditional daisies,

00:07:22.438 --> 00:07:24.357
(Laughter)

00:07:24.399 --> 00:07:26.067
and we got yelled at.

00:07:26.818 --> 00:07:28.361
Go figure.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.863
I thought about law school,

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:33.533
thinking perhaps of becoming a paralegal.

00:07:33.533 --> 00:07:37.996
And then I completed a program
on dispute resolution.

00:07:39.205 --> 00:07:41.082
It all went nowhere.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.502
I love to write.

00:07:44.544 --> 00:07:49.215
So I created a program
called Getting Started on Your Memoirs.

00:07:49.716 --> 00:07:54.345
That program has met
with "limited success."

00:07:54.345 --> 00:07:55.513
(Laughter)

00:07:56.055 --> 00:07:58.433
It's been a rocky road for me, too.

00:07:58.474 --> 00:08:00.309
And I told you to buckle up.

00:08:00.351 --> 00:08:01.436
(Laughter)

00:08:01.477 --> 00:08:03.813
Now, I know all this can sound bad.

00:08:04.439 --> 00:08:07.692
But it's really important
to keep trying and experimenting

00:08:07.734 --> 00:08:12.363
with different activities that'll make you
want to get up in the morning again,

00:08:12.405 --> 00:08:13.990
because if you don't,

00:08:14.032 --> 00:08:18.077
there's a real good chance
of slipping back into phase two,

00:08:18.077 --> 00:08:20.705
feeling like you've been hit by a bus,

00:08:20.747 --> 00:08:22.957
and that is not a happy prospect.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.337
Not everyone breaks through to phase four.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.966
But those who do are some
of the happiest people

00:08:32.008 --> 00:08:34.093
I have ever met.

00:08:34.802 --> 00:08:39.599
Phase four is a time
to reinvent and rewire.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:45.438
But phase four involves answering
some tough questions, too.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.648
Like what's the purpose here?

00:08:47.648 --> 00:08:49.442
What's my mission?

00:08:49.484 --> 00:08:53.696
How can I squeeze all the juice
out of retirement?

00:08:54.655 --> 00:08:57.033
You see, it's important
that we find activities

00:08:57.075 --> 00:08:58.743
that are meaningful to us

00:08:58.785 --> 00:09:01.537
and that give us
a sense of accomplishment.

00:09:02.163 --> 00:09:07.710
And my experience is that it almost
always involves service to others.

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:13.216
Maybe it's helping a charity
that you care about.

00:09:13.925 --> 00:09:17.845
Maybe you'll be like the old coots.

00:09:17.887 --> 00:09:21.307
Yeah, these folks took a booth
in the local farmers market

00:09:21.349 --> 00:09:24.811
and were prepared to give their advice

00:09:24.852 --> 00:09:28.689
based on their vast years of experience

00:09:28.689 --> 00:09:30.858
to anyone who came by.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.820
Or maybe you'll be like my friend Bill.

00:09:34.487 --> 00:09:38.908
I met Bill a few years ago
in a 55-plus activity group.

00:09:39.617 --> 00:09:42.078
In the summer, we golf together

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:45.039
and walk together and bicycle together,

00:09:45.039 --> 00:09:46.999
and in the winter we curl.

00:09:47.041 --> 00:09:52.004
But Bill had this idea that we should
exercise our brains as well.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:55.216
He believed that there was
a tremendous pool

00:09:55.216 --> 00:09:58.928
of expertise and experience in our group.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.055
And so he approached a number of folks

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:04.767
and asked if they would volunteer
to teach some of the things

00:10:04.809 --> 00:10:07.228
that they love to do to others.

00:10:07.562 --> 00:10:10.606
And almost invariably they agreed.

00:10:11.274 --> 00:10:13.651
Bill himself taught two sessions,

00:10:13.693 --> 00:10:17.989
one on iPads and one on iPhones.

00:10:18.447 --> 00:10:20.741
Because we were smart enough to know

00:10:20.741 --> 00:10:24.328
that a number of our members
had been given these things

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:29.083
as gifts at Christmas by their children

00:10:29.125 --> 00:10:31.419
and that they barely knew
how to turn them on.

00:10:31.419 --> 00:10:32.962
(Laughter)

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.216
The first year we offered nine programs

00:10:37.258 --> 00:10:40.094
and there were 200 folks signed up.

00:10:40.761 --> 00:10:45.433
The next year, that number
expanded to 45 programs

00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:48.895
with over 700 folks participating.

00:10:49.478 --> 00:10:53.691
And the following year
we offered over 90 programs

00:10:53.691 --> 00:10:57.236
and had 2,100 registrations.

00:10:57.653 --> 00:10:58.821
Amazing.

00:10:58.821 --> 00:11:03.701
(Applause)

00:11:03.701 --> 00:11:05.745
That was Bill.

00:11:06.662 --> 00:11:10.166
Our members taught us to play
bridge and mahjong.

00:11:10.208 --> 00:11:11.709
They taught us to paint.

00:11:11.751 --> 00:11:14.837
They taught us to repair our bicycles.

00:11:14.837 --> 00:11:18.216
We tutored and mentored local school kids.

00:11:18.257 --> 00:11:23.638
We set up English as a second language
programs for newcomers.

00:11:23.971 --> 00:11:27.558
We had book clubs, we had film clubs,

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.852
we even had a few golf clubs.

00:11:29.894 --> 00:11:33.147
Exhausting, but exhilarating.

00:11:33.773 --> 00:11:36.817
That's what's possible in phase four.

00:11:38.069 --> 00:11:40.571
And do you remember the five losses

00:11:40.613 --> 00:11:43.199
that we talked about in phase two?

00:11:43.199 --> 00:11:47.328
Loss of our routine and identity
and relationships

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.497
and purpose and power.

00:11:49.538 --> 00:11:52.792
In phase four, these are all recovered.

00:11:52.833 --> 00:11:55.378
It is magic to see.

00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:56.796
Magic.

00:11:56.837 --> 00:12:02.885
So I urge you to enjoy
your vacation in phase one.

00:12:03.761 --> 00:12:05.638
(Laughter)

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.934
Be prepared for the losses in phase two.

00:12:10.685 --> 00:12:14.772
Experiment and try as many
different things as you can

00:12:14.814 --> 00:12:16.399
in phase three.

00:12:16.691 --> 00:12:20.486
And squeeze all the juice
out of retirement

00:12:20.486 --> 00:12:21.654
in phase four.

00:12:21.696 --> 00:12:23.698
(Applause)

